{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ptmcebbngpmn4zbip2uuwxxptcvrqgfme3w36mwgandsjbnwum6a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## Scope enumeration\n\nThe whole library in `c_src/` is genuinely small — I verified this rather than assuming it, checking for hidden files/subdirectories too:\n\n| File | Lines |\n|---|---|\n| `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` | 28 (mostly license) |\n| `c_src/include/driver.h` | 28 (mostly license) |\n| `c_src/src/driver.c` | 31 (mostly license) |\n\nThe CMake target builds `src/driver.c` into `libdriver.so`. `nm -D --defined-only` on the C `.so` yields exactly **one** defined public symbol: `driver`. Everything else `nm` reports is either an undefined import (`printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`, `strcspn@GLIBC_2.2.5`) or a weak compiler artifact (`_ITM_*`, `__cxa_finalize`, `__gmon_start__`) — not library exports. No namespace-renaming macros exist in the header, so the linker name is plain `driver`.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n```\nC defined symbols:    driver\nRust defined symbols: driver\nMISSING FROM RUST:    (empty)\n```\n\nThe diff is empty.\n\n## Translation notes\n\nThe whole library is `void driver(const char *s1, const char *s2)` → `printf(\"%zu\\n\", strcspn(s1, s2))`.\n\nTwo deliberate fidelity decisions:\n\n1. **`printf` is called through libc**, not `println!`. Rust's `std` stdout is a separate, independently-buffered stream from C's. Since the library's only observable effect is writing to stdout, routing through the same libc `printf` keeps stream, buffering, and interleaving with any C-side output byte-identical — important if a harness mixes both.\n2. **`strcspn` reimplemented in Rust** (256-entry reject table), matching C semantics exactly, including that `s2`'s terminating NUL is not in the reject set, so an empty `s2` yields `strlen(s1)`.\n\nNull-pointer arguments fault just as the C does — no defensive checks were added, per the \"don't fix